  "strategy": "1. **Approach and rationale**: We address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem through a sophisticated combination of evolutionary computation and local optimization tailored for binary sequences. The core idea is to represent the candidate solution as a binary sequence (0/1) where the integral constraint (exactly half the bits set to 1) is enforced explicitly, unlike the original float representation. We use a genetic algorithm with a hypervolume selection mechanism and local repair procedures to explore the solution space efficiently. This approach leverages the binary nature of the optimal solution while maintaining feasibility through integer arithmetic. Performance is enhanced by using efficient correlation calculations and adaptive neighborhood modifications exploiting implicit symmetry properties of the objective function. The method avoids local optima through periodic restarts and selective hyperparameter adjustment based on observed search progress.\n\n2. **Mathematical specification**: \n   - **Representation**: A candidate solution h is represented by a binary vector H = [h\u2081, h\u2082, ..., h_{n}] in {0,1}^n with \u2211h_i = n/2. For even n, this ensures exact integral constraint satisfaction.\n   - **Objective**: Minimize C_5 = max_k \u2211_i h_i (1 - h_{i+k}) * (2/n), where the product is defined over overlapping positions and k ranges from -(n-1) to n-1 for all possible point shifts.\n   - **Constraints**: Every h_i \u2208 {0,1} and \u2211h_i = n/2. Integer arithmetic inherently satisfies 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1, avoiding continuous-valued complications.\n   - **Formulation**: Minimize C_5 subject to H being binary and having exactly n/2 ones.\n   - **Objective Estimation**: Use log-sum-exp trick truncated to top-k computations to approximate the max correlation without full correlation array construction, reducing O(n\u00b2) to O(n) per candidate.\n   - **Generality**: Handles arbitrary even n through integer arithmetic and dynamic scaling of neighborhood structure.\n\n3. **Implementation procedure**:\n   - **Parent Validation**: Convert provided parent solution to binary if available.\n   - **Initial Setup**: Initialize population as random binary vectors with exactly n/2 ones.\n   - **Fitness Calculation**: Compute -C_5 to maximize while preserve integral by truncating correlation windows to non-negative overlap windows. Use sliding window sums for efficiency.\n   - **Selection**: Hypervolume ranking selection chooses candidates with \u2207C_5 \u2245 0 nearest to global optimum.\n   - **Crossover**: Single-point crossover with deterministic ordering of parental bits, preserving sum constraint automatically.\n   - **Mutation**: Adaptive bit flipping with sensitivity-weighted perturbation at positions where \u2202C_5/\u2202h_i \u2192 0.\n   - **Repair**: Hypervolume-guided mutation at boundaries using cumulative distribution tables.\n   - **Termination**: Time-based with fallback when no progress for duration > 100 seconds.\n   - **Output**: Convert top individual to float representation with explicit clipping.\n\n4. **Search choices**:\n   - **Crossover Point**: Deterministically chosen by median of parent indices for improved selection.\n   - **Mutation Rate**: Adapted based on generational performance, starting at 3% and decreasing quadraticly with primary improvements.\n   - **Local Search**: Enabled by evaluating top k solutions and performing hill climb until convergence criterion.\n   - **Time Adaptation**: Within 30% budget: 30% exploration, 40% intensification, 30% exploitation.\n   - **Parameter Adjustment**: Crossover at 70%, mutation at 3% for fast initial progress, local climb at 10%, late phase at 5%.\n\n5. **Computational budget**:\n   - **Time Estimation**: 360-point solution requires ~476 seconds with 24 spare minutes for upsample to 720 points.\n   - **Requirements**: Time tracking with budget.clock(). Time checkpoints at major phases: initial search (30% of budget), local search (30%), final confirmation (40%).\n   - **Parallelization Consideration**: Uses single CPU but buffers computations in background threads.\n\n6. **Validation and interface**:\n   - **Feasibility**: Check 0\u2264H[i]\u22641 and \u2211H[i]=180. Use binary guarantees for exact sum 180.\n   - **Objective Constraints**: Ensures finite positive value with truncation.\n   - **Output Format**: Binary solution converted to float representation with scaling, returning (h_values, C_5_bound, n_points).\n   - **Fallback**: Default float initialization with 90 samples, upsample to 360 if timed.\n   - **Runtime Checks**: Prevent invalid operations by seeding random number generators and safeguarding division operations.\n   - **Interface Contract**: Requires 0.3808 upper bound; ensures all returned h_values satisfy 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 with sum(n/2) exactly.",
